  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Seventy uncut diamonds vanished from a locked vault with zero witnesses. This happened in London's Hatton Garden back in 1913, inside the most heavily guarded merchant district in Europe.",
        "visual_query": "london vintage street 1910s",
        "visual_fallback": "old city street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The courier delivered a heavy lead-sealed package directly to the chief merchant's desk. The ledger was signed, the receipt was stamped, and the box was slid onto the top shelf of the safe.",
        "visual_query": "vintage bank ledger paperwork",
        "visual_fallback": "old documents"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Yet when the safe was opened twenty minutes later, investigators found no scratches on the heavy steel lock and no tunnels beneath the floorboards. How do you steal a fortune when the door was never even picked?",
        "visual_query": "heavy vintage steel vault door",
        "visual_fallback": "metal safe"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The twist is that the theft happened long before the box ever touched the vault. Police tracked a corrupt courier who had systematically swapped the parcel out for lead weights weeks earlier during transit.",
        "visual_query": "vintage postal courier bag",
        "visual_fallback": "leather bag"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The missing gems were already melted down in a back-alley jeweler's shop three days before the alarm was raised. The perfect crime wasn't magic\u2014it was just paperwork.",
        "visual_query": "vintage goldsmith workshop interior",
        "visual_fallback": "workshop tools"
      }
    ],
